ry regions and critical residues of NOD2 involved in muramyl dipeptide recognition. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>23</volume>:<fpage>1587</fpage>&#x02013;1597.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15044951</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Vavricka, S.R., M.W. Musch, J.E. Chang, Y. Nakagawa, K. Phanvijhitsiri, T.S. Waypa, D. Merlin, O. Schneewind, and E.B. Chang. <year>2004</year>. hPepT1 transports muramyl dipeptide, activating NF-kappaB and stimulating IL-8 secretion in human colonic Caco2/bbe cells. <source>Gastroenterology.</source> <volume>127</volume>:<fpage>1401</fpage>&#x02013;1409.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15521010</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Watanabe, T., A. Kitani, P.J. Murray, and W. Strober. <year>2004</year>. NOD2 is a negative regulator of Toll-like receptor 2-mediated T helper type 1 responses. <source>Nat. Immunol.</source> <volume>5</volume>:<fpage>800</fpage>&#x02013;808.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15220916</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>